# meta-deploy.list: the queue meta-deploy.bash runs, in this order.
#
# STANDING RULE (CLAUDE/AgentNotes.md): this list is exactly what needs running NOW. Add a
# run when it is pending, remove it once it has run and is done, leave out what cannot
# usefully run yet, then ask the owner to run meta-deploy.bash. Every step a run needs lives
# in the plan's own scripts, so this is the only command the owner is handed. The order is by
# dependency. A plan with none of triage.bash, deploy.bash and acceptance.bash has nothing
# for the script to do. A triage-only plan is run once, read-only. Words after the folder
# name are passed to that plan's triage.bash, both runs.
#
# One entry per line, four kinds:
#   <plan folder>                  its triage, deploy and acceptance scripts, in order. The
#                                  folder is named as it sits in CLAUDE/Plan/, with no
#                                  CLAUDE/Plan/ in front (00123-name, not CLAUDE/Plan/00123-name)
#   playbooks/<path>.yml [args]    a change no plan owns; run as one unit through its own
#                                  shebang, which goes through run.bash, exactly as
#                                  ./playbooks/imports/<play>.yml does by hand. Words after
#                                  it are ansible-playbook arguments: name the play's tags
#                                  (--tags lightning) so only the changed part runs. A
#                                  tagged run never decides the play's state, so a later
#                                  changed-plays still runs the play in full if it is due
#   <plan folder>/<script>.bash    one plan script, for a step none of the three fixed names
#                                  fits; run as one unit, like a playbook entry, except
#                                  that a script named *acceptance*.bash may exit 2 for
#                                  COULD NOT ESTABLISH, as an acceptance.bash may
#   changed-plays                  every play that has to run again here (one that has run
#                                  here, or one with no ledger record whose files here
#                                  show it ran), as one unit: the checkout's
#                                  ./run.bash --changed --yes, in playbook-main.yml order,
#                                  stopping at the first failure; the other plays with no
#                                  record (NEVER, UNJUDGED) are named and never run
#
# changed-plays runs WHERE IT STANDS in the list, like every other entry, and decides which
# plays it runs when it starts, so the entries above it have already run (a play an entry
# above ran is no longer changed, and is not run twice). Put it above the plan entries whose
# deploy or acceptance expects the changed plays to have run, which is the usual case, and
# below an entry it must follow. It replaces a hand-copied list of stale plays: name a
# playbooks/ entry only for a play that must run at a fixed point between two plan steps.
# Its description says which changed plays the agent expects it to pick up and why.
#
# A line starting with # is a comment and is the description of the entry below it: it is how
# the owner learns what a run will do, so write it. Blank lines are ignored. Any other line
# that is not an entry is a fatal error naming its line number.

# Plan 00257 B.5 and its reviews' fixes (now at ccy 3.122.32, container 2.84.38): the teamchat
# host play installs the new teamchat-admin and member program, the health unit's text, and
# re-applies every present team (a new member program widens the run): each terminator is
# restarted onto the new program and its request gate, and team apply writes the health unit's
# drop-in of the listen addresses. The new teamchat-tls@.service runs each terminator with
# LimitNOFILE=2112: it holds 1024 connections, at most 256 from one source address by default
# (lower where a team declares connections_per_source). A connection over either bound is
# refused at once; nothing is reclaimed, and a dead connection (a suspended member host) is freed
# by the 2-minute idle limit. teamchat-ctl sends the admin token only to the homeserver unit's
# own system cgroup.
# WARNING: team apply now judges each declared network whole (HOST-7). A team whose network has
# a longer route inside it through another interface (a broad VPN prefix that holds a podman or
# LXC bridge subnet, say) is refused with exit 70 naming the network and both interfaces, though
# it applied before; that team is left as it was and the play fails. The fix is in host_vars:
# split the declared network so no part of it is routed through another interface, or remove
# the overlapping route; then run this again.
# Plan 00257 B.12 (owner: no LXC member session starts with its container): lxc apply rewrites
# each v2 LXC seat's session unit with no [Install] and Restart=no, and where an earlier apply
# made the seat's wants link it removes the link and STOPS that running session. From now on a
# v2 LXC seat's session starts only on demand (teamchat-admin lxc restart --member).
# Plan 00209, the v2 batch (ccy 3.124.9, container 2.84.60), its member program and kit:
# - Task 5.1 step 2 (the member tag): the host kit is placed whole by its own program
#   (teamchat kit assemble) at a new digest directory, with the plugin, hooks, skill and settings
#   a host cc `run --claude` needs. It is assembled beside that directory and renamed into place,
#   and the play fails unless every part is there before the kit's current link moves; a run that
#   stopped part way is repaired by the next. The program is kept beside the kits as
#   /usr/local/lib/teamchat/teamchat-member-<digest>.
# - Task 5.1 steps 3 and 7: teamchat run takes --extra-settings (host cc's deny rules), and reach
#   reads a --state session's seats.
# - Task 4.6, seat duty: a team's approved `duties` (coordinator, worker, host) reach each
#   session's desired membership, and `teamchat whoami --json` reports each held seat's duty.
# - Backlog R0, the questions verbs: ask-human, thread (--note), close, retitle, bump,
#   questions --waiting/--for/--digest and verify --room questions; the teamchat skill teaches them.
#   Every non-parked member joins the questions room (only humans and the coordinator post), and a
#   human's answer is relayed to the seat a question was asked for.
# Nothing running changes until a session starts.
playbooks/imports/play-teamchat.yml
# The ccy image is rebuilt once for the teamchat it carries; nothing a session does changes.
# The same run installs issue #288's Go commands (ccy 3.122.26: ccy-sessions, ccy-teams and the
# launcher's front), whose typed-line submitter no longer takes a screen with no input box as
# entered and names what the box showed when a line stays in it. Its ccy-teams (ccy 3.122.34,
# container 2.84.40) adds Start its session in its LXC container to an LXC member's menu, shown
# only on this host's LXC member whose entry has service: true (Plan 00257 B.12). It also
# deploys Plan 00258's watchlist command (full clones and study runs; internal/gitx, which ccy
# links, changed). Running sessions keep the old ccy until they restart.
# Plan 00209, the v2 batch (ccy 3.124.9, container 2.84.60): host cc --teams joins teamchat
# teams. The run installs the new ccy-teams (internal cc-run, the host chooser and consent on
# teamchat), cc, cc-teams.bash, cc-claude, and cc-teams-settings.json with the host lane's deny
# rules: no tests, builds or heavy QA (qa-all, qa-changed, qa-go-qa and the other test or build
# gates) in any host cc --teams session, agent-bus ones included, whatever the seat's duty;
# qa-deployed-drift.bash stays allowed (Plan 00263 D3). The image is rebuilt once for its
# teamchat, which carries seat duty, whoami and the R0 questions verbs above. A cc or ccy session
# already running keeps the old launch until it is started again.
playbooks/imports/play-claude-yolo.yml
# Plan 00257 B.12: the v1 agent-bus play rewrites each LXC member's agent-bus-claude-<user>
# unit (service: true) with no [Install] and Restart=no, and DISABLES AND STOPS one an earlier
# run enabled, so a running v1 LXC member session ends here. It never starts one again: the
# only start is ccy-teams, Enter on the member's row, Start its session in its LXC container
# (installed by play-claude-yolo.yml above).
playbooks/imports/play-agent-bus.yml
# Plan 00257 B.12 proof. Its checks are read-only: in each declared LXC member container that
# is running, the session units are present, not enabled and not linked in
# multi-user.target.wants. A stopped container is reported offline and skipped. It then ASKS,
# per running container, whether to reboot it (default no; asked at the terminal even under
# meta-deploy) and reboots only on the owner's yes, then checks no claude runs for its members'
# users. Exit 2 means it could not establish the answer: nothing judged (every container
# offline, say), fewer units found than members declared, a probe that did not answer, or a
# failed lxc-stop or lxc-start.
00257-cross-host-lxc-team-members/lxc-no-autostart-acceptance.bash
# Plan 00209 Task 5.1 acceptance, read-only, its unattended part: teamchat version answers,
# the host kit is whole (program, settings, plugin manifest, hooks and skill), and the deployed
# ccy is this checkout's CCY_VERSION (3.124.9). It asks nothing here and exits 0 when those
# hold. It lists as ATTENDED, and does not judge, what needs a team, a checkout or a person:
# ccy-teams system saying teamchat in the checkout, the session's conversation record, and the
# launch, round trip, stop, restore and kill of DESIGN-host-cc-v2.md section 8. Those are run
# by hand afterwards, at a terminal, with <team> <checkout> [<session>].
00209-agent-bus-v2-in-go/acceptance-host-cc-v2.bash
# Q15 (last, since it can remove the passwordless sudo the runs above may need): passwordless sudo is now the host's declared choice, applied by this new last play.
# Run it only while this host's environment/localhost/host_vars/localhost.yml declares
# `passwordless_sudo_for_unattended_deploys: true` (this host's unattended deploys need
# passwordless sudo). With it, the play keeps the /etc/sudoers grant as it is and removes
# /etc/sudoers.d/99-fedora-desktop-setup if a kickstart left one. Without the line, or with
# `false`, it REMOVES passwordless sudo, and meta-deploy.bash can no longer run unattended here.
playbooks/imports/play-ZZZ-passwordless-sudo.yml
